{"kind":"article","sections":[],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Custom Dimensions and Units","modules":[{"name":"Math"}]},"abstract":[{"text":"Extend ","type":"text"},{"code":"Math","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with your own physical dimensions and units—currency, points, gameplay metrics, or anything else your app measures.","type":"text"}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CustomDimensions","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math"]]},"seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Extending-the-Framework","generated":true,"title":"Extending the Framework","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"Overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"Math","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ships with a comprehensive set of base dimensions (","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/length","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", and friends) and derived dimensions (","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/speed"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/energy"},{"text":", and more). These cover physics and everyday measurement, but your app almost certainly measures things that aren’t in the SI system too: money, hit points, ingredients, resource points, users, and so on.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The good news: ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" is an open system. Because a dimension is ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"just a dictionary of exponent powers","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", you can describe any dimension you can imagine at runtime.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Define a Custom Dimension","level":3,"anchor":"Define-a-Custom-Dimension","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Create a ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension"},{"text":" with a dictionary of exponent identifiers. Each key is a dimension name and each value is its power:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["import Math","","\/\/ A dimension for money, with a single base exponent.","let usdDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"USD\": 1])","","\/\/ A dimension for \"users\", used later in per-user metrics.","let userDimension = PhysicalDimension(exponents: [\"user\": 1])"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"You can build compound custom dimensions with the same arithmetic used","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"internally: ","type":"text"},{"code":"+","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" multiplies dimensions, ","type":"text"},{"code":"-","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" divides them, and ","type":"text"},{"code":"*","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" with an","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"Int","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" raises a dimension to a power:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let revenuePerUser = usdDimension - userDimension   \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"user\": -1]"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"level":3,"text":"Define Custom Units","type":"heading","anchor":"Define-Custom-Units"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Custom dimensions don’t have a compile-time type, so use"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/MathDimension\/unknown","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" as the type-level parameter and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/NamedUnit","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"create units. Pass a "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/LinearConverter","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (for a simple scale) or an"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/OffsetConverter","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" (for a scale plus an offset) to handle conversions:"}]},{"code":["let usdUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"$\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.0)",")","","\/\/ EUR is defined relative to the same base dimension.","let eurUnit = NamedUnit<MathDimension.unknown>(","    symbol: \"€\",","    dimension: usdDimension,","    converter: LinearConverter(coefficient: 1.09)",")"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"name":"Tip","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep the same "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"PhysicalDimension"},{"type":"text","text":" value (here "},{"code":"usdDimension","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") for every","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"unit in a family. The framework only converts between units whose dimensions","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"match, so sharing the exact same value makes your money interchangeable."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"type":"aside","style":"tip"},{"level":3,"text":"Convert Between Custom Units","type":"heading","anchor":"Convert-Between-Custom-Units"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom units convert exactly like the built-in ones:","type":"text"}]},{"code":["let walletInEur = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: eurUnit)","let walletInUsd = walletInEur.converted(to: usdUnit)","","print(walletInUsd.value) \/\/ 109.0"],"type":"codeListing","syntax":"swift"},{"level":3,"type":"heading","text":"Combine Custom Units with Built-In Ones","anchor":"Combine-Custom-Units-with-Built-In-Ones"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Custom dimensions participate in dimensional arithmetic too. Here a currency","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"dimension combines with the built-in "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" dimension:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let hourlyRate = Quantity(value: 50.0, unit: usdUnit) \/ Quantity(value: 1.0, unit: Units.hour)","","print(hourlyRate.value)          \/\/ 50.0","print(hourlyRate.unit.dimension) \/\/ [\"USD\": 1, \"time\": -1]","print(hourlyRate.unit.symbol)    \/\/ \"($\/h)\""],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"Use the Currency Dimension","anchor":"Use-the-Currency-Dimension"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If your custom dimension happens to be currency, ","type":"text"},{"code":"Math","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" already defines a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/currency","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" dimension and a built-in library of world","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"currencies (and cryptocurrencies) in ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":". Currency units automatically","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"place their symbol before the value when formatting:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["let gift = Quantity(value: 100.0, unit: Units.usd)","print(gift.formatted()) \/\/ \"$100.00\"","","\/\/ Resolve units from ISO 4217 codes at runtime.","if let currency = Units.currency(for: \"SEK\") {","    print(currency.symbol) \/\/ \"kr\"","}"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"What’s Next?","anchor":"Whats-Next"},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Learn how to lock custom units into a compile-time dimension in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Explore ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/Units","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":" to see the breadth of units provided out of the box.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]}]}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/math\/customdimensions"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://Math/documentation/Math/CompileTimeSafety":{"title":"Compile-Time Dimension Type Safety","kind":"article","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/math\/compiletimesafety","abstract":[{"text":"Use generic constraints to make sure your APIs only ever receive the right kind of quantity.","type":"text"}],"role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/CompileTimeSafety"},"doc://Math/documentation/Math/PhysicalDimension/time":{"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"The dimension of time (SI base unit: second).","type":"text"}],"title":"time","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"static","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"var","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"time","kind":"identifier"},{"text":": `Self`","kind":"text"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Math\/documentation\/Math\/PhysicalDimension\/time","url":"\/documentation\/math\/physicaldimension\/time"},"doc://Math/documentation/Math/PhysicalDimension":{"title":"PhysicalDimension","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"PhysicalDimension"}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/math\/physicaldimension","abstract":